# Lab notebook writing

## Voice

Write as someone discovering dynamical systems for the first time (curious beginner energy from ~15 years ago), not as a textbook or product blog.

- First-person or plain “you/we” is fine; no lecturer “one observes that…”
- Concrete: what you saw on the canvas, what surprised you, what you tried next
- Uneven is OK; perfect parallel three-beat lists are not
- Brand: Classical Chaos; keep math honest (equations, named constants)

## Required passes before shipping a post

1. **Copyright check** — skill `copyright-check` (facts vs expression; no close paraphrase; assets OK)
2. **AI filter** — skill `ai-prose-filter` (ban-list + structure tells; rewrite flagged lines)
3. **Public reader** — skill `public-reader-check` (fresh agent; must-fixes applied unless review-only)

Do not mark a post done until 1–2 report clear (or fixes are applied) and the public-reader report has been shown.

## Drafting basics

- Prefer facts-only bullets → rewrite in a clean context (no source article pasted into rewrite)
- Record embed vs full-canvas **affordances** before any UI claims
- Cite discoverer/year when it helps; use our thumbs/`VizEmbed`, not others’ figures
- **Never** name repo paths, filenames, or internal module APIs (`utils/…`, `createAttractorPage`, `foo.tsx`). Readers do not have the repo. Show algorithms as plain snippets; link the live canvas instead.
- End-to-end build: skill `blog-builder`
- Voice / craft only: skill `lab-notebook-writing`
